  "prompt": "Which methods did Socrates employ to challenge the prevailing thoughts of his time?",
  "answer": "Socrates challenged the prevailing beliefs of his time through several related methods:\n\n- **Probing questions:** He asked people to define familiar ideas—such as justice, courage, and virtue—and explain why they believed what they did.\n- **Elenchus, or cross-examination:** He tested their answers with further questions, exposing contradictions between their claims. This encouraged people to reconsider beliefs they had taken for granted.\n- **Professed ignorance:** Rather than presenting himself as an authority, he often claimed not to know the answer. This stance, sometimes called *Socratic irony*, prompted others to explain and defend their views.\n- **Dialogue and counterexamples:** He examined ideas through conversation, using concrete examples to test whether a proposed definition held up in different situations.\n- **Ethical self-examination:** He urged people to examine their lives and prioritize virtue and the care of the soul over wealth, reputation, or power.\n\nHis aim was to move beyond inherited opinion toward beliefs that could withstand rational scrutiny, even when a discussion ended in uncertainty rather than a settled answer.",
